Benchmark: Open-Slot Reconstruction Assay

Question: Does the context-weighted compiler make better, better-calibrated use of open symbols and missing context than a single-meaning or lexical-only baseline?

Design

• Build a small, consented set of Pantheon carriers with recoverable source context, plus carriers where original intent is genuinely unrecoverable.
• Mask selected slots (actor, action, source, timing, cause, or intended operation). Keep ground truth sealed until after interpretation.
• For a subset, preserve identical carrier text while varying title, adjacent artifacts, modality metadata, or self/other action provenance (Metamer Assay).
• Compare: (A) lexical-only single reading; (B) context-only ranking; (C) context-weighted, evidence-linked branching with abstention.
• Blind human reviewers to condition. Ask them to choose or rank interpretations, identify unsupported claims, and state what evidence would change their choice.
• Split by source artifact or topic, not random rows, so near-duplicate material does not leak across train and holdout sets.

Measures

• Top-k match to recoverable source truth, reported separately from useful task interpretation.
• Calibration and Brier score for confidence on recoverable slots.
• Abstention quality when the source truth is missing or not inferable.
• Unsupported-claim rate and correct identification of counterevidence.
• Interpretation stability under irrelevant title/style changes; appropriate change under relevant new evidence.
• Task completion and time-to-useful-action, with permission and accuracy gates scored separately.

Falsification conditions

The new pass is not an improvement if it only produces more interpretations, raises confidence without better calibration, or increases unsupported claims. It should be revised if the added action/modality metadata does not improve held-out interpretation or task performance over simpler baselines.

